// 🌀 1. LOOPS: "Repeat Until Done"
// 💡 Definition:
// Loops are like a robot that repeats your task again and again, until you tell it to stop.

// 🔧 Purpose:
// When you want to do something many times, like greeting 5 people, printing numbers from 1 to 100, or going through a list of names.

// 📦 Types of Loops (with daily-life example and code):
// 🔁 for loop — Best for counting
// Imagine you want to say Hello to 5 people:


for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log("Hello Person", i);
 }
// 🧠 Think of it like:

// “Start from 1, go till 5, move one step at a time → Do this job.”

// 🔄 while loop — Best for doing until a condition fails
// Example: “Keep eating pizza while you’re hungry.”


 let hungry = true;
 let slices = 3;

 while (slices > 0) {
   console.log("Eating pizza...");
   slices--;
 }
// Do it while condition is true.

// 🔂 do...while — Do it once at least, then check.

 let num = 1;

 do {
   console.log("Printed at least once");
   num++;
 } while (num <= 3);
// 🗺️ 2. MAP — "Make New Copy with Changes"
// 💡 Definition:
// .map() is like a magic copy machine 🖨️ that transforms each item in a list and gives you a brand-new list.

// 🔧 Purpose:
// Use it when you want to change/modify every item in an array and get a new array.

// 📦 Example:
// Imagine you have prices in dollars and you want to convert them to rupees:


 const dollars = [1, 5, 10];
 const rupees = dollars.map((d) => d * 278);
 console.log(rupees); // [278, 1390, 2780]
//  You mapped each dollar to rupees!

// 🧹 3. FILTER — "Pick What You Want"
// 💡 Definition:
// .filter() is like a sieve or strainer that removes what you don’t want and keeps only the matching items.

// 🔧 Purpose:
// Use it to choose items that pass a test/condition.

// 📦 Example:
// Pick even numbers from a list:


 const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
 const evens = nums.filter((n) => n % 2 === 0);
 console.log(evens); // [2, 4]
// Like “keep only the numbers that are even.”

// 🧮 4. REDUCE — "Summarize Everything"
// 💡 Definition:
// .reduce() is a collector that takes a whole list and squashes it into one single value (sum, product, etc.)

// 🔧 Purpose:
// When you want one final result from a list (like total, average, product).


const numss = [1, 2, 3, 4];
const total = numss.reduce((sum, n) => sum + n, 0);
console.log(total); // 10







// | Method     | Mnemonic 💭                 | Real-Life Example            | Use When...                       |
// | ---------- | --------------------------- | ---------------------------- | --------------------------------- |
// | **for**    | Count from A to B           | Greet 10 people              | You know how many times           |
// | **while**  | Do until condition is false | Keep eating till you’re full | You don’t know how many times     |
// | **map**    | Modify All & Return New     | Convert dollars → rupees     | You want to **change all** values |
// | **filter** | Keep What You Want          | Keep even numbers            | You want to **choose** items      |
// | **reduce** | Combine All into One        | Sum total money              | You want **one final result**     |
